# 23. Explain to an Interviewer — 60-Second Script 🎤

> For classification models, I don't rely only on accuracy because it can be misleading, especially with imbalanced datasets. I start with the confusion matrix to understand true positives, true negatives, false positives, and false negatives. Precision tells me how reliable positive predictions are, while recall tells me how many actual positive cases the model detects. I use F1-score when I need a balance between precision and recall. For threshold-independent evaluation, I consider ROC-AUC, and for rare positive classes I often examine the Precision-Recall curve and PR-AUC. The final metric and classification threshold should be selected based on the real-world cost of different errors.

---

# 24. Top 3 Interview Questions 🎯

## Q1. Why can a model have 99% accuracy but still be useless?

**Answer:**

> On an imbalanced dataset, the model can achieve high accuracy by always predicting the majority class while completely failing to detect the minority class. That's why we examine precision, recall, F1-score, and other suitable metrics.

---

## Q2. Precision vs Recall?

**Answer:**

> Precision measures how often positive predictions are correct, while recall measures how many of the actual positive cases the model successfully detects. Precision is important when false positives are costly, while recall is important when false negatives are costly.

---

## Q3. When would you use PR-AUC instead of ROC-AUC?

**Answer:**

> PR-AUC is often more informative when the positive class is rare because it focuses directly on precision and recall for the positive class. ROC-AUC can sometimes look strong even when performance on rare positive cases is not practically sufficient.

